    "result_text": "The full suite is green. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change adds a `namesakeCount` to owners: at create time, the number of existing owners sharing the same first + last name (case-insensitive), returned on the create response and on later reads.\n\nThe acceptance test creates the owner, then reads it back via `GET /api/owners/{id}` and expects `namesakeCount` there \u2014 so the value must be **persisted at create time**, not computed on the fly. I implemented it that way:\n\n**New pipeline step** \u2014 `AssignOwnerNamesakeCount.java`: counts `ownerRepository.findAll()` entries whose first and last name match the new owner's (via `equalsIgnoreCase`), and stores it on the owner. Placed after `assignCustomerCode`, before `save`, so the count reflects owners already stored (the new owner isn't persisted until the final `save`, mirroring how `AssignOwnerCustomerCode` already relies on this ordering).\n\n**Wiring** \u2014 `owners.POST.yml`: inserted the `assignNamesakeCount` step (with `govern: [ transaction ]`) between `assignCustomerCode` and `save`.\n\n**Model/schema** \u2014 added a persisted `namesakeCount` (`namesake_count INTEGER`) field to `Owner`, with column added to the hsqldb (test) and h2 schemas, plus a trailing `NULL` on the positional seed inserts in `hsqldb/data.sql` (h2 uses named columns, so it needed no change). MapStruct's `OwnerMapper` maps the field automatically, so both the create responder and the GET responder return it.\n\n**API contract** \u2014 added the read-only `namesakeCount` (int32, min 0) property to the `Owner` schema in `openapi.yml`, which generates the `OwnerDto` field.\n\nResult: `AcceptanceTest.coreCountsNamesakes` and the full suite pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\nNote: I scoped the schema changes to hsqldb and h2, matching where the prior `customer_code`/`household_id` columns live; the mysql/postgres schemas already lack those earlier columns and were left as-is.",
